# Bevy ECS Best Practices

## Control Flow

### Prefer `match` over nested `if/else`
Match statements make branches explicit and exhaustive. Use `let-else` for early returns.

```rust
// ❌ Avoid nested if/else
if let Some(x) = foo {
    if let Some(y) = bar {
        // deeply nested
    } else {
        return;
    }
} else {
    return;
}

// ✅ Prefer let-else for early returns
let Some(x) = foo else { return; };
let Some(y) = bar else { return; };
// flat code continues

// ✅ Use match for multiple branches
match (drag_state.is_dragging, intersection.point) {
    (true, Some(point)) => handle_drag(point),
    (false, Some(point)) => handle_hover(point),
    (_, None) => clear_state(),
}
```

### Reset state with Default trait
When clearing multiple fields, prefer `Default::default()` or struct update syntax:

```rust
// ❌ Verbose field-by-field clearing
intersection.point = None;
intersection.ray = None;
intersection.is_within_bounds = false;

// ✅ Use Default for clean resets
*intersection = FrustumNearPlaneIntersection::default();
```

## Systems & Scheduling

### Prefer focused systems with run conditions
Small systems with `run_if` conditions are more efficient than large systems with internal branching.

```rust
// ✅ Skip entire system when preconditions not met
.add_systems(
    Update,
    (
        handle_intersection.run_if(any_with_component::<ProbeCamera>),
        handle_drag.run_if(resource_changed::<NearPlaneDragState>),
        draw_visualization.run_if(in_state(ProbeToolState::Probing)),
    )
        .chain(),
)
```

### Use SystemSets for ordering
Group related systems and define ordering at the set level:

```rust
#[derive(SystemSet, Clone, Debug, Hash, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ProbeSystemSet {
    Input,
    Processing,
    Visualization,
}

app.configure_sets(Update, (
    ProbeSystemSet::Input,
    ProbeSystemSet::Processing.after(ProbeSystemSet::Input),
    ProbeSystemSet::Visualization.after(ProbeSystemSet::Processing),
));
```

## Events & Messages

### Always drain EventReaders
Events not read will accumulate. Drain every frame, even conditionally:

```rust
// ✅ Always read events
fn handle_commands(mut events: EventReader<Command>) {
    for event in events.read() {
        match event {
            Command::Add { .. } => { /* handle */ }
            Command::Remove { .. } => { /* handle */ }
        }
    }
}
```

### Use enum variants for related commands
Group related operations in a single event type:

```rust
// ✅ Related commands in one enum
#[derive(Event)]
pub enum ProbeCameraCommand {
    Add { transform: Transform, resolution: UVec2 },
    Remove { entity: Entity },
    UpdateSettings { entity: Entity, settings: KernelSettings },
}
```

### Separate input from output events
Commands flow in, notifications flow out:

```rust
// Input commands
pub enum ProbeCameraCommand { Add { .. }, Remove { .. } }

// Output notifications  
pub enum ProbeCameraOutput { KernelChanged { .. }, CameraSpawned { .. } }
```

## Observers vs Events

### Use Observers for entity-scoped reactions
Observers trigger on specific entities; events broadcast globally:

```rust
// ✅ Observer - entity-targeted
fn on_readback_complete(
    trigger: On<ReadbackComplete>,
    query: Query<&ProbeConfig>,
) {
    let entity = trigger.entity;
    let Ok(config) = query.get(entity) else { return };
    // React to this specific entity
}

// ✅ Event - broadcast communication
fn handle_commands(mut events: EventReader<Command>) {
    // React to any matching command
}
```

| Pattern | Use For |
|---------|---------|
| Observer (`On<T>`) | Entity lifecycle, GPU callbacks, component reactions |
| Events | Commands, user input routing, cross-system communication |
| `Changed<T>` | Reacting to component value changes |

## Components & Markers

### Use marker components for query filtering
Markers enable efficient query filtering and run conditions:

```rust
// ✅ Marker enables targeted queries
#[derive(Component)]
pub struct ProbeCamera;

// Query only probe cameras
Query<&Transform, With<ProbeCamera>>

// Run condition
.run_if(any_with_component::<ProbeCamera>)
```

### Use `#[require(...)]` for component dependencies
Auto-attach related components:

```rust
#[derive(Component)]
#[require(Camera, Camera3d, Transform)]
pub struct ProbeCamera {
    pub resolution: UVec2,
}
```

### Avoid redundant markers
Don't create markers that duplicate queryable state:

```rust
// ❌ Redundant - the component itself is the marker
#[derive(Component)]
struct HasKernelBindGroup;

// ✅ Just query for the component
Query<Entity, With<KernelBindGroup>>
```

## Queries

### Use `Single<>` for guaranteed single-entity queries
When exactly one entity should match:

```rust
// ✅ Clearer intent, better error messages
fn system(window: Single<&Window>, camera: Single<&Camera, With<MainCamera>>) {
    // Guaranteed single entity
}
```

### Define QueryData for complex repeated queries
Reduce boilerplate and improve consistency:

```rust
#[derive(QueryData)]
#[query_data(mutable)]
pub struct ProbeCameraQuery {
    pub entity: Entity,
    pub transform: &'static mut Transform,
    pub camera: &'static Camera,
    pub projection: &'static Projection,
}

fn system(mut query: Query<ProbeCameraQuery>) {
    for probe in query.iter_mut() {
        // Access probe.entity, probe.transform, etc.
    }
}
```

### Filter with `With<>`/`Without<>` over runtime checks
Let the ECS do the filtering:

```rust
// ❌ Runtime filtering
fn system(query: Query<(&Transform, Option<&ProbeCamera>)>) {
    for (transform, probe) in query.iter() {
        if probe.is_some() { /* ... */ }
    }
}

// ✅ Query-level filtering
fn system(query: Query<&Transform, With<ProbeCamera>>) {
    for transform in query.iter() {
        // Only probe cameras
    }
}
```

## Change Detection

### Use `Changed<T>` for mutation reactions
Only run when component actually changed:

```rust
fn on_settings_changed(
    query: Query<(&KernelSettings, &mut Pipeline), Changed<KernelSettings>>,
) {
    for (settings, mut pipeline) in query.iter_mut() {
        pipeline.rebuild(settings);
    }
}
```

### Cache expensive computations
Store derived data in components rather than recomputing:

```rust
#[derive(Component)]
pub struct CachedFrustumCorners {
    pub corners: [Vec3; 8],
    pub last_computed: f32,  // time
}

fn update_cache(
    mut query: Query<(&Projection, &mut CachedFrustumCorners), Changed<Projection>>,
) {
    // Only recompute when projection changes
}
```
